# VegaLoad load testing

This rule is for real load: many users, longer runs, shapes, thresholds and
baselines. For a quick check that an API works under a few users, use the
`vegaload-smoke` rule first.

`vegaload init` registered VegaLoad's MCP server for this project (see
`.cursor/mcp.json`). It exposes nine tools, each a thin wrapper around a
`vegaload` CLI command you could run yourself in a terminal:

- `create_scenario` — scaffold a scenario file (`vegaload new`). The
  scenario's `http`/`ws` globals can make real calls, carrying a value from
  one into the next, under the same per-call allowlist described below.
  Scripts can also call `check(value, {name: test})` to count named
  assertions without failing the iteration; gate on them with the
  `check_rate` threshold metric. A threshold can target one step
  (`p95{step="login"} < 300ms`), one protocol (`p95{protocol="ftp"} < 500ms`)
  or one operation (`error_rate{protocol="ftp",op="roundtrip"} < 1%`).
- `run_test` — run a load test against a scenario file or a protocol-direct
  target (`target` + `protocol`). Choose `executor`: `fixed-vus`, `ramp`,
  `step`, or `constant-arrival-rate`. Pass `baseline_path` (and `max_regression`,
  in percent) to compare the run with an earlier report; the result then has
  `baseline.passed`. Pass `data_files` (CSV or JSON, read in the scenario as
  `data.NAME.next()`, or as `{data.NAME.COLUMN}` on each call for postgres, mysql, redis, ftp, sftp, kafka and rabbitmq; env stays for scenario files), `env` and `secret_env` (read as `env.NAME`; use
  `secret_env` for tokens, it removes the value from all output). Pass `junit_path` to also write JUnit XML (one test case per
  threshold, check and baseline gate). Returns the run's results and, unless
  `no_report` is set, a path to a self-contained HTML report.
  A non-localhost target that isn't in `allow_targets` is refused unless
  `yes: true` is set — this MCP server has no terminal to prompt a human on,
  so treat `yes` as a deliberate choice, and confirm with the user before
  sending load at anything that isn't localhost or a target they named.
- `get_results` — read back a previously-written report JSON file. It includes
  `protocols` when the run recorded calls. A script HTTP 4xx or 5xx counts as
  a success in that row.
- `suggest_thresholds` — suggest p95 latency and error-rate ceilings from a
  baseline run's report.
- `diagnose_failure` — rule-based findings on a report (failure rate, latency
  long tail, whether failures were transient), plus an LLM narrative if
  `VEGALOAD_LLM_PROVIDER` is configured for this MCP server.
- `compare_reports` — diff a candidate JSON report against a baseline
  (`vegaload compare`); reports whether error rate, p95 latency or a check's
  pass rate regressed. It also lists each protocol's p95 and error rate.
  Those lines do not change the verdict.
- `generate_from_spec` — write a runnable scenario (every operation as a named
  step) and a runbook of `vegaload run` commands from a JSON OpenAPI spec (JSON
  only, not YAML). The order is a guess: validate it and edit it.
- `list_protocols` — list every supported protocol, with options and examples
  (`vegaload protocols`). Call it before choosing a `protocol` value.
- `validate_scenario` — run a scenario once, with one user, and report whether
  it works (`vegaload validate`). Makes real calls under the same host rules as
  `run_test`. Returns `valid`, and when not valid, `stage` and `error`. Call it
  before `run_test` on any new or edited scenario.

Typical flow: generate_from_spec (if an OpenAPI spec was given) → validate_scenario → run_test →
get_results / diagnose_failure → suggest_thresholds once a baseline looks
healthy → compare_reports after a later run. Summarize results in plain
language (total requests, failure rate, p95 latency) and mention the HTML
report path rather than pasting raw JSON.

## The live view

A `run_test` is also shown live in the user's browser by default (the live view):
the page opens by itself, and its address comes back as `live view: URL`, the
second text item of the result (the first is always the result JSON). After you
start a run, tell the user that URL. Pass `no-live` only if they do not want it,
and `no-open` to keep it from opening a browser tab. It is off in CI.
